Every clinician should conduct a focused-history during a pain-evaluation.
If a clinician conducts a focused-history during a pain-evaluation then the clinician should include a pain-history in the focused-history and should include a pain-characteristic in the focused-history and should include a potential-contributing-factor in the focused-history.
A potential-contributing-factor-example includes a function-example and includes a work-history-example and includes a current-work-demand-example and includes a psychosocial-stressor-example and includes a sleep-example.
Every clinician should conduct a physical-examination during a pain-evaluation.
If a clinician conducts a pain-evaluation and a diagnostic-testing is not indicated during the pain-evaluation then it is recommended that the clinician does not conduct the diagnostic-testing during the pain-evaluation.
If a clinician conducts a pain-evaluation and a diagnostic-testing is indicated during the pain-evaluation then the clinician may conduct the diagnostic-testing during the pain-evaluation.
A diagnostic-testing-indication-example includes a severe-or-progressive-neurologic-deficit-example and includes a suspected-serious-underlying-condition-example.
